Bayin stands on other people's work. This file says whose.

CHIMERA
  A physical-modelling voice synthesizer by Jameson Hodge.
  https://github.com/jamesondh/chimera-vocal-synth, MIT License (LICENSES/CHIMERA-MIT.txt).
  Used unchanged, as a dependency pinned in Cargo.toml. From it come:
    - the simulation of air in tubes: sections, openings, walls, radiation, turbulence noise
    - the sound sources: the vocal fold model, the lip valve, the whistle
    - the whole human voice: anatomy, articulators and their limits, calibrated vowels
    - the creatures: songbird, seal, alligator, elephant, with their parameters and calls
    - the mammal airway the dog is built on
    - deterministic math and the decimation filter
  Without CHIMERA there would be no sound in Bayin.

What is Bayin's own
  The Instrument, Spec and Player layer; the bugle, air horn, steam whistle and dog, each
  assembled from CHIMERA's parts; the space and wave-steepening stages; the measuring and
  calibration tools; the dataset format and tool.

Reference recordings
  The recordings Bayin's instruments are measured against are other people's, on Wikimedia
  Commons. They are not in this repository. Each one, with its author and license, is listed in
  references/SOURCES.md.

Ideas
  Treating an instrument as actions that become sound, to be learned by a world model, follows
  Music-JEPA: Z. Wang, K. Fang and Y. LeCun, "Music-JEPA: Learning a World Model of Sound from
  Action", arXiv:2607.22000, 2026.
